  "evidence": "TEXT_C: Six Brexit tests restoring legal supremacy and sovereignty; net migration to ~zero with Australian-style points and five-year contributions before benefits/NHS; “British jobs first”; +20,000 police, restore stop and search, deport foreign criminals, repeal Human Rights Act/leave ECHR; NHS/social care +£11bn and mental health +£500m; ban face coverings and disband sharia councils; animal-welfare measures. TEXT_F: NHS +£30bn and creation of a National Care Service with +£8bn; free university tuition and restored maintenance grants; £10 Living Wage, ban zero‑hours, rights from day one, sectoral bargaining; build over 1m homes incl. 100k social homes/year, cap rent rises and three‑year tenancies; free primary school meals and expanded childcare; uphold refugee obligations and guarantee EU nationals’ rights; jobs‑first Brexit retaining SM/CU benefits; defence ≥2% and renew Trident; fairer tax with top 5%/large firms paying more and crackdown on avoidance.",
  "reasoning": "Given the profile’s highest weights on Care/Harm and strong Equality, TEXT_F delivers broader, more concrete harm‑reduction and equality measures (major NHS/social care funding, workers’ rights, housing, education, and child welfare) while still offering some loyalty signals (jobs‑first Brexit, NATO/Trident). TEXT_C concentrates on loyalty/authority (sovereignty, migration near‑zero, policing, ECHR exit) and some proportionality (contribution before benefits), but offers comparatively less expansive care/equality provisions, so overall aligns less with the weighted priorities.",
  "confidence": "medium",
  "winner": "TEXT_F"
}
